From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui/cocoa: no light line around a borderless window

What: a window switched to borderless (the tests' full screen over a
display, OMACVM_TEST_SKIP_DISPLAYS, for the main window and the other
displays' windows) has no shadow; leaving that full screen gives the main
window its shadow back. One helper, omacvm_set_borderless, makes a window
borderless and drops the shadow.

Why: macOS 26 draws a light 1 pt rim (white at about 20 %, a fainter third
pixel) with a window's shadow, just inside the window's edges. QEMU's
window is made titled and later switched to borderless with setStyleMask,
which keeps its shadow, so a borderless window over the whole display had
a light line on all four edges, over the strip beside the notch too
(MacBook Air, macOS 26.6.2, 2026-10-07: 2.9.1's notch full screen during a
benchmark round; the same code is 3.0.x's test-mode full screen). The
line is gone from a capture of the window without its shadow, so it is
WindowServer's, not QEMU's or the guest's. macOS's own full screen (real
use since 3.0.0) has no shadow and no rim; a titled window keeps both.

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's borderless full screen).
Test: Tests/display/test-borderless-rim.sh (build time, on the patched
ui/cocoa.m: no borderless switch outside the helper, the shadow given back;
--self-test catches 3.0.3's code) and test-borderless-rim-live.sh (pixels,
on a Mac with macOS 26 or newer: a borderless window's edge with and
without the helper). Applies after omacvm-cocoa-tap-permission.patch.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -2911,6 +2911,20 @@
     [cocoaView omacvmFullScreenTake];
 }
 static NSWindowStyleMask notchSavedMask;
+static BOOL notchSavedShadow;
+
+/*
+ * A window without a frame has no shadow. macOS 26 draws a light 1 pt rim
+ * (white at about 20 %) with a window's shadow, just inside its edges; on a
+ * borderless window over a whole display that rim is a line around the
+ * screen. A window made titled and then borderless keeps its shadow, so
+ * every borderless switch drops it here.
+ */
+static void omacvm_set_borderless(NSWindow *w, NSWindowStyleMask extra)
+{
+    [w setStyleMask:NSWindowStyleMaskBorderless | extra];
+    [w setHasShadow:NO];
+}
 
 /*
  * OmacVM tests only (OMACVM_TEST_SKIP_DISPLAYS): the borderless full screen,
@@ -2925,7 +2939,8 @@
         notchFull = YES;
         notchSavedFrame = [w frame];
         notchSavedMask = [w styleMask];
-        [w setStyleMask:NSWindowStyleMaskBorderless | NSWindowStyleMaskResizable];
+        notchSavedShadow = [w hasShadow];
+        omacvm_set_borderless(w, NSWindowStyleMaskResizable);
         [w setFrame:[target frame] display:YES];
         [w orderFront:nil];
         [fullScreenMenuItem setTitle:@"Exit Full Screen"];
@@ -2936,6 +2951,7 @@
     } else {
         notchFull = NO;
         [w setStyleMask:notchSavedMask];
+        [w setHasShadow:notchSavedShadow];
         [w setFrame:notchSavedFrame display:YES];
         [fullScreenMenuItem setTitle:@"Enter Full Screen"];
         [cocoaView updateScale];
@@ -7794,7 +7810,7 @@
         NSRect r = NSMakeRect(frame.origin.x + in.left, frame.origin.y + in.bottom,
                               frame.size.width - in.left - in.right,
                               frame.size.height - in.top - in.bottom);
-        [hd->window setStyleMask:NSWindowStyleMaskBorderless];
+        omacvm_set_borderless(hd->window, 0);
         [hd->window setFrame:r display:YES];
         [hd->window orderFront:nil];
         hd->want_full = false;
